[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, and impossible predicates as first-order design failures. Fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable, faithful version before tuning difficulty.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build relations must be realizable by the actual deck and layout; exotic or mismatched predicates are effectively no rule at all.\n- Repair the move graph before balancing challenge. Universal 0-move starts, near-zero usage, or tiny exhausted searches usually mean active piles lack a common opening transfer or cannot reach any progress route.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: restore one coherent source \u2192 tableau \u2192 sink loop first, so playtests measure gameplay structure instead of parser errors or disconnected rules.\n\n## Align card flow with victory\n- If the win condition requires tableau or other active piles to empty, the game needs a reachable irreversible sink: foundations, removals, completed-set cash-outs, or another one-way destination. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying objective.\n- The win condition should measure real resolution of the play area, not depletion of bookkeeping sources like stock/draw or temporary holding zones.\n- If a pile can receive cards but not release them, it must either count as completed work or be kept out of the critical flow. One-way receiving piles that the objective does not reward usually become dead sinks, blockers, or loopholes.\n- Sink capacity and structure must match the deck and objective. Too few destinations, over-restrictive opening rules, or sinks that cannot absorb the whole required workload can make a game mathematically unwinnable.\n- Prefer incremental, steady progress sinks over \u201call-at-once\u201d completion gates. Designs are more solvable when ordinary exposed cards can convert organization into durable advancement.\n- Every populated pile type should have a clear role in the win path: expose cards, reorganize them, or accumulate irreversible progress.\n\n## Shape mobility and search space\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use points to missing mobility or disconnected flow; search-cap losses with moderate-to-high use usually point to excessive reversible freedom or missing irreversible progress.\n- When branching is high but wins are absent, tighten the busiest reversible move routes before adding more freedom. Restricting empty-space use, removing backflow from progress areas, or making destinations encode stronger structure often improves solvability.\n- Add mobility in broad, structural ways, not with many exceptions. One useful tableau transfer, stack move, or limited helper space is usually better than several special-case escape hatches.\n- Favor movement rules that reinforce the game\u2019s main structure. Stack moves should preserve the tableau\u2019s build logic, and tableau rules should organize cards toward the sink rather than create aimless circulation.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should either support reorganization temporarily and be clearable again, or be repurposed into constrained completion zones.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small, compatible changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in otherwise workable games, improve opening mobility with the narrowest broadly available rule that fits the design, such as a simple tableau relation or rank-limited move to empties.\n- If the game already has a real sink but play feels too linear or awkward, add one general reorganization route among active piles before loosening the sink itself.\n- Small layout shifts can matter when rules are sound: increasing early tableau capacity or reducing stock gating can improve organization and reveal the real decision space, but only if the stock actually feeds play.\n- When a game already has a natural accumulation area, prefer aligning the win condition to that progress route over adding new subsystems. Reuse existing meaningful pile roles before inventing more mechanics.\n\n## Express rules cleanly\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine dislikes duplicate source/destination rules, merge related permissions into one clear condition tree or broader source set instead of scattering near-duplicate rules.\n- Use syntax fixes to clarify structure, not just to silence errors: after every compile repair, re-check that each rule still contributes to a coherent progression system.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solitaire shows lots of search but no wins, look for whether the main active piles have enough irreversible exits. Expanding an existing sink so many more exposed cards can enter it is often a better repair than adding more tableau mobility, because it converts churn into progress instead of just increasing branching.\n2. If most losses are fully exhausted with near-zero card usage, the first fix is usually not \u201cmore freedom\u201d but a valid progress loop: a broad reorganization route plus an irreversible sink that directly aligns with the win condition.\n\nWhen a receiving pile type already exists, repurposing it into a true completion area is often a cleaner repair than adding a brand-new subsystem.\n3. When a solitaire has occasional fully exhausted near-zero-move losses, the problem is often the opening graph rather than overall difficulty. A small, globally available opening rule\u2014especially one tied to a meaningful rank like Kings to empties\u2014can remove dead starts while also reducing degenerate free movement compared with unrestricted empty-pile fills.\n4. Before tuning difficulty or strategy, make the layout arithmetically valid with the smallest possible count correction. A minimal deck-balance fix preserves the current move structure, so the next round\u2019s playtest data reflects gameplay problems rather than setup errors.\n5. If a win condition requires active piles to empty, first verify that the irreversible sinks have enough total capacity to absorb the whole deck. An otherwise reasonable layout can be unwinnable simply because there are too few destination piles to hold all required progress.\n6. When losses consistently hit search limits with moderate pile/card usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of incremental irreversible progress. Replacing an all-at-once completion gate with a foundation-like sink usually improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding extra rearrangement moves.\n7. When a solitaire asks the play area to empty into foundation-like sinks, the sink capacity has to match the amount of card mass the player must retire. If playtests show lots of movement but zero wins, first check whether the irreversible destinations are numerous and open enough to absorb the whole deck.\n8. If a win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb that workload. A tiny or partially blocked sink turns otherwise interesting rearrangement rules into aimless search, because play can reorganize cards without ever converting that organization into lasting progress.\n9. When a compact solitaire has some wins but many very short, fully exhausted losses, the problem is often the opening layout rather than the move rules. A tiny increase in starting exposure or tableau occupancy can rescue dead starts without flattening the game\u2019s core decisions.\n10. When a solitaire has moderate card/pile usage but no wins and non-exhaustive searches, the problem is often excessive reversible movement rather than insufficient freedom. Tightening the main tableau relation so that ordinary moves create stronger structure can improve solvability more effectively than adding more helper spaces or extra move types.\n11. If the win condition is \u201cempty the tableau,\u201d the design needs a sink that irreversibly absorbs cards and is itself part of the victory path; otherwise even elegant rearrangement rules can only shuffle deadlock around. When a pairing rule is too sparse to open play reliably, a small, thematic opening valve (like rank-limited moves to empty spaces) is often enough to make deals playable without collapsing difficulty.\n12. When a solitaire variant fails to compile, fix the smallest structural issue that restores a complete card flow before changing gameplay rules. Early playtest data is only meaningful once every card is actually placed and each pile\u2019s intended role can be observed in play.\n13. If the only path to victory is moving cards into a sink pile type, that sink must have enough parallel capacity and opening opportunities to absorb the whole deck. A mathematically undersized or rarely-openable sink makes the game unwinnable no matter how much tableau mobility you add.\n14. When a game\u2019s victory depends on emptying active piles, make sure there is enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the whole deck; otherwise the layout may be mathematically unwinnable no matter how much tableau mobility you add. A good first repair is often to align the win condition with the sink the rules already support, before changing movement structure.\n15. When a variant fails before playtesting, fix exact card accounting and restore one coherent card-flow loop before changing difficulty. A minimal compile repair is more informative than a larger redesign, because it lets later simulation data reveal whether the gameplay structure itself works.\n16. When a solitaire shows high pile usage but no wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of durable progress. A receiving pile that is supposed to be the main sink should accept cards in a natural incremental sequence from common exposed ranks, or the game will devolve into reversible shuffling instead of resolution.\n17. When a game already has a one-way receiving area, it is often better to turn that area into the actual progress sink and align the win condition to it than to keep demanding that active tableau piles empty with no irreversible destination. A rearrangement network without a rewarded sink tends to produce either impossible deals or endless search churn.\n18. When a game uses a reduced deck whose suits all share the same color, \u201calternate color\u201d and \u201csame color\u201d predicates behave very differently from a full deck: one may become impossible while the other becomes broadly permissive. Check how suit/color predicates collapse under the actual deck composition before using them as core movement rules.\n19. If a pile can receive cards but cannot release them, either make it a rewarded completion area or keep it out of the critical path. One-way side piles that the win condition still expects to empty tend to create huge amounts of misleading search without adding meaningful decisions.\n20. If the win condition asks the tableau to empty, the design needs a destination that turns organization into irreversible progress; otherwise the game becomes pure rearrangement or dead-end trapping. When a receiving pile already exists, it is often better to convert it into a true sink and align the win condition with it than to add an entirely new subsystem.\n21. When a ruleset already has a one-way accumulation area, the safest first repair is often to align victory with filling that area rather than inventing a new sink. Also, if a special source like a draw pile cannot be referenced in win logic, express completion through the sizes or emptiness of regular piles that collectively account for the whole deck.\n22. When a rules engine rejects duplicate source/destination move definitions, merge them into one condition tree instead of splitting \u201csame move, different source pile\u201d across multiple rules. Also, if one pile type is clearly the temporary workspace and another is the accumulation area, the win condition should reward emptying the workspace rather than a helper pile that can only hold one card at a time.\n23. If simulations show long search-cap losses with moderate pile usage, look first for pile types that can receive or hold cards without contributing to victory. Converting stranded populated piles into part of the main tableau, and making the win condition track the piles where real organization happens, often improves both solvability and meaningful decision-making more than adding raw mobility elsewhere.\n24. When a solitaire repeatedly explores large state spaces without finding wins, the problem is often excess reversible freedom rather than insufficient mobility. Tightening the main rearrangement rule so it preserves the same structure needed by the progress sink can reduce churn and make organization translate into actual advancement.\n25. When a rules engine rejects overlapping move definitions, merge them into one condition tree before changing the game\u2019s design; a clean compile is the fastest way to learn whether the underlying card flow works at all.\n26. When using duplicated suits or decks, sink capacity has to scale with the duplicated sequences you expect players to complete. If the foundation structure can absorb only one copy of a suit-run but the deck contains two, some deals become impossible no matter how much tableau mobility you add.\n27. When a game has a genuine progress sink, allowing easy backflow out of that sink can turn progress into pure churn and overwhelm simple solvers. A small restriction that makes advancement irreversible often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding extra mobility.\n28. When a one-way receiving area is the only irreversible sink, its capacity must match the amount of material the win condition expects the player to clear. If the sink can hold only part of the deck, playtesting often shows high usage and deep searches but no wins because the game can organize cards without ever being able to finish.\n29. When many shuffled starts are proven unwinnable early, the issue is often opening mobility rather than overall difficulty. A narrow relaxation on single-card access to empty tableau spaces can revive dead starts while preserving challenge if stronger stack moves remain constrained.\n30. When a layout undercounts the deck, the best repair is usually to place the missing cards into a pile with a clear gameplay role rather than just inflating existing piles. A small stock/draw that feeds active piles can restore validity while preserving the original tableau structure for evaluation.\n31. When a rules engine rejects overlapping move definitions, fix the conflict by consolidating permissions into one broader, coherent rule rather than duplicating source\u2192destination pairs. Minimal compile repairs are most informative when they preserve the existing card-flow structure, so the next playtest measures gameplay rather than parser issues.\n32. When a game has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, the problem is often not mobility but that its irreversible sink is mismatched to the deck. A completion area should be easy enough to enter incrementally and have enough capacity structure to absorb the whole deck, or the rest of the game becomes aimless churn.\n33. When many shuffled deals die in just a handful of fully explored moves, the problem is often missing opening transfers rather than overall challenge. A small rule that broadens ordinary tableau connections while keeping empty spaces constrained can raise solvability without collapsing the game into unrestricted shuffling.\n34. When a solitaire\u2019s only irreversible progress requires completing a full long sequence at once, most deals become mathematically or practically dead even if reordering is allowed. Adding a compatible incremental sink usually improves both solvability and decision-making more effectively than adding more tableau mobility alone.\n35. When a solitaire already wins on every tested shuffle with high card and pile usage, the problem is often not missing flow but excess freedom. Tightening a single high-leverage permission\u2014especially how empty spaces can be reused\u2014can add decision pressure without disrupting the game\u2019s overall card flow.\n36. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search caps with moderate card/pile usage but no wins, the problem is often excessive reversible freedom rather than insufficient mobility. Tightening the busiest non-progress move so it encodes real structure can improve solvability more than adding new moves.\n37. When playtests show high move counts, decent pile usage, and zero wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of irreversible progress. A one-way destination should usually start empty and function as a rewarded completion sink; if it begins clogged with random cards, it tends to become dead weight rather than part of the win path.\n38. When playtests show high move counts and broad pile usage but no wins, the problem is often excessive reversible circulation rather than lack of mobility. A good repair is to make the progress piles unambiguously irreversible and clean up the opening layout so every occupied pile serves either reorganization or permanent advancement, not both.\n39. When a solitaire consistently hits search limits with moderate card usage but no wins, the problem is often excessive reversible motion rather than insufficient legal moves. Converting a loosely-constrained helper pile into a one-way progress sink can both reduce aimless branching and align play with an empty-the-tableau win condition.\n40. When playtests show high search counts and decent pile usage but no wins, the problem is often not lack of movement but lack of irreversible progress. Converting an awkward or low-capacity sink into a steady accumulation route usually helps more than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n41. If a game\u2019s losses fully exhaust after only a handful of moves with near-zero card usage, the problem is usually not \u201cdifficulty\u201d but a broken progress route: the tableau may move, but the sink cannot actually accept what the deck produces. Repair the irreversible path from active piles to the win condition before tuning mobility or adding complexity.\n42. When many losses are fully exhausted after only a handful of moves, the bottleneck is often opening mobility, not late-game difficulty. A narrow relaxation on single-card moves into empty spaces can unfreeze deals without giving away the whole game, especially if stronger multi-card movement remains constrained.\n43. When a solitaire file fails to compile, fix the smallest accounting or syntax defect first before rebalancing rules; otherwise playtest data says nothing about the design itself. In particular, after any pile-count edit, recompute the full deck total explicitly rather than assuming the rest of the layout still balances.\n44. If a solitaire\u2019s victory depends on emptying the active tableau into foundations, the number and type of foundation piles must be able to absorb the entire deck under the build rules. A game can look mobile on paper yet be mathematically unwinnable simply because its irreversible sink has too little capacity.\n45. When many losses are exhausted in 0\u20132 moves while wins already use the full layout, the core issue is usually opening mobility, not endgame structure. A narrow relaxation that creates more legal first moves\u2014especially around empty-space use\u2014can rescue dead deals without flattening the rest of the game.\n46. When a pile type is a one-way receiver, the win condition should treat filling it as progress rather than demand a later exact configuration that ordinary play cannot reach. If most losses are fully exhausted with near-zero usage, first check whether the sink/objective relationship is mathematically compatible before adding more mobility.\n47. When a solitaire already has decent card usage but most losses hit the search limit, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than insufficient freedom. Tightening the most permissive rearrangement rule\u2014especially empty-pile movement\u2014can improve decision quality and solvability more than adding new moves.\n48. When a solitaire prototype fails to compile because cards are unassigned, fix deck accounting first with the smallest layout change that preserves the intended roles of stock, tableau, and sink; otherwise playtest data says nothing about the design. Also check sink-opening predicates while repairing structure\u2014an accidental missing rank can make an otherwise valid game effectively unwinnable.\n49. When a solitaire already has a one-way accumulation area, make the objective recognize that progress before adding new mechanics. Search-heavy losses with decent card usage often mean the game has movement, but its irreversible sink is either ignored by the win condition or structurally disconnected from winning.\n50. If every active pile starts occupied, \u201cmove only to empty\u201d is usually equivalent to \u201cno game at all\u201d; give the tableau at least one broadly available relation that can occur in random openings.\nWhen the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, add an irreversible sink sized to the deck\u2019s workload; pure rearrangement cannot win by itself.\n51. When a solitaire variant fails to compile because cards are unassigned, prefer the smallest count correction that preserves the existing move graph and pile roles. Restoring a valid full deck first gives playtest data about gameplay structure instead of obscuring everything behind setup errors.\n52. When playtests show high move counts and moderate card usage but no wins, the problem is often not mobility but lack of incremental irreversible progress. Replacing an all-at-once completion rule with a steady sink usually improves both solvability and decision quality without needing a full redesign.\n53. When a solitaire already has a working sink and decent win rate, search-cap losses often come from overly flexible temporary storage rather than lack of mobility. Tightening helper spaces with a simple rank gate can preserve interesting rescue plays while cutting reversible branching enough to improve practical solvability.\n54. When a ruleset depends on suit-color relationships, verify that the chosen deck actually contains the needed color diversity. A build rule that is theoretically standard but incompatible with the deck composition behaves like no mobility rule at all, causing many deals to be dead on arrival.\n55. If the objective is to empty active tableau piles, the game needs enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the whole deck; a single constrained receiving pile often makes the puzzle mathematically unwinnable even when local moves look rich.\n\nWhen losses come with long searches plus some proven unwinnable deals, first check whether the progress area can actually accept all required cards before