urns a non-runnable design into a testable one.\n62. When a solitaire file fails to compile, fix the smallest structural inconsistency first\u2014especially exact card accounting\u2014before changing gameplay rules. A runnable but imperfect game yields useful playtest data; an invalid one yields none.\n63. When a win condition depends on emptying active piles, the irreversible sink must have enough independent capacity to absorb the whole deck under its own start rules. If most losses are fully exhausted and provably impossible, first check whether the sink count/opening requirements make completion mathematically too narrow before adding more mobility.\n64. When simulations show lots of card/pile usage but zero wins, the issue is often not lack of mobility but lack of reachable irreversible progress. Replacing an all-at-once completion gate with an incremental foundation or sink can turn aimless rearrangement into meaningful advancement without rewriting the whole game.\n65. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with high card and pile usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of irreversible progress. Converting a reversible \u201chelper\u201d area into a one-way accumulation sink and aligning the win condition to that sink can reduce churn while making ordinary organization moves matter.\n66. If a solitaire win condition requires active piles to become empty, the rules need an irreversible destination that can absorb cards permanently; pure rearrangement rules, even if plentiful, cannot produce a win state. When a helper area is already present, repurposing it into a constrained progress sink is often a cleaner repair than adding an entirely new subsystem.\n67. When losses split between immediate dead starts and long, fruitless searches, the usual culprit is \u201crearrangement without resolution\u201d: players can move cards around, but too little of that motion converts into permanent progress. A good fix is often to add an incremental one-way sink whose capacity matches the whole deck, rather than a tiny all-or-nothing completion pile.\n68. When a solitaire file fails basic deck accounting, fix that exact structural error before tuning difficulty; otherwise playtest feedback says more about the broken setup than the game. Minimal compile repairs are especially valuable when the intended correction is already implied by the file\u2019s own comments.\n69. When a solitaire has moderate card/pile usage but only search-cap losses, the problem is often reversible branching rather than lack of moves. Tightening helper piles into genuinely temporary spaces while adding one structure-preserving tableau mobility rule can reduce churn and make progress routes more legible.\n70. When a solitaire already has a valid irreversible sink, allowing routine moves back out of that sink often hurts more than it helps: it inflates search space, turns progress into churn, and can reduce practical solvability even if it increases nominal mobility.\n71. When a solitaire can rearrange cards freely but asks for all active piles to become empty, it needs at least one clearly irreversible sink that is excluded from the emptiness workload or structurally represents completion. Converting an existing high-traffic pile type into a one-way progress area is often a more informative fix than adding yet more tableau mobility.\n72. When a solitaire regularly reaches the search cap with moderate-to-high card usage, the problem is often excessive reversible freedom rather than insufficient moves. Restricting how empty spaces can be filled is a strong, compact way to cut churn while still preserving meaningful tableau decisions.\n73. If the win condition requires active piles to empty, first check the sink\u2019s total practical capacity, not just whether a sink exists. A one-way destination that can accept only a small subset of cards makes the game mathematically unwinnable even when ordinary tableau moves seem fine.\n74. Before tuning difficulty, fix deck accounting with the smallest coherent setup change you can. A runnable but imperfect layout teaches far more than a clever rules rewrite on top of a file that never compiles.\n75. When a solitaire already has decent usage and partial solvability but losses mainly hit search limits, the problem is often excessive reversible freedom rather than lack of moves. Tightening the busiest tableau-to-tableau rule into a more directional organizing structure can improve both solvability and decision quality without making the sink itself easier.\n76. When a game\u2019s losses split between immediate dead starts and long search-cap churn, the usual culprit is a weak progress sink: players can move cards around, but not convert that motion into durable advancement. A small redesign that gives the sink enough capacity and a clear opening rule often helps more than adding extra mobility.\n77. When a solitaire variant fails to compile, fix the smallest structural fault first\u2014especially exact card accounting\u2014before tuning rules or difficulty. A minimal repair preserves the current design\u2019s signal, so the next playtest reveals gameplay problems instead of setup noise.\n78. When a solitaire asks the player to empty active piles, it needs enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the whole workload. A partially populated or undersized foundation system can create lots of legal motion without any viable route to victory, producing search-heavy losses instead of meaningful challenge.\n79. When the win condition requires clearing active piles, first check whether the sink can actually absorb the full workload. In multi-deck games, foundation-style sinks often need one destination per suit-copy; otherwise the game may allow lots of motion while being mathematically unable to finish.\n80. When a win condition requires tableau piles to empty, first check whether the game\u2019s irreversible sinks have enough total capacity to absorb every card that must leave play. If sink count or opening constraints cap progress below the deck size, most losses will be proven quickly because the game is mathematically blocked, not merely difficult.\n81. When a game shows lots of movement but no wins, the missing ingredient is often not more mobility but a more incremental irreversible sink. Requiring all-at-once completion can create endless reorganization; allowing ordinary exposed cards to convert into durable progress usually improves both solvability and decision quality.\n82. Before tuning difficulty, make the lightest possible setup repair that restores exact deck accounting and preserves the intended card-flow structure. A compile-fix that also avoids changing rules gives cleaner evidence about whether the gameplay loop itself works.\n83. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with moderate card usage, the problem is often too much reversible motion and too little meaningful progress. A good fix is usually to turn any existing \u201creceiver\u201d piles into a true one-way sink that aligns with the win condition, while making empty tableau spaces more constrained so they create planning value instead of infinite churn.\n84. If the objective is to empty active tableau piles, the game needs an irreversible sink that can absorb the full deck in ordinary play. Temporary holding or partial-capacity receiving piles may create movement, but without enough true progress capacity they produce large search spaces with no actual route to victory.\n85. When a game already wins often but some losses are proven impossible, look for sink rules that blur \u201cprogress\u201d and \u201crearrangement.\u201d Making completion piles more one-way and structurally stricter can both reduce search noise and eliminate self-inflicted dead states without a full redesign.\n86. If the win condition asks active piles to empty, at least one destination in the move graph must act as irreversible progress rather than temporary storage. Reusing an existing pile type as a constrained sink is often a better first repair than adding more free movement, which can increase search without creating any path to victory.\n87. If a win condition requires clearing active piles, first verify that the game has enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb every card that must leave them. A sound rearrangement rule set cannot compensate for too few completion piles or for sinks that can only accept a fraction of the deck.\n88. When a receiving pile has no outgoing moves, it must represent irreversible progress in the win condition rather than something that also needs to be emptied. If the objective is to clear active piles, provide enough sink capacity for the whole deck and let that sink count as completion.\n89. When a game has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink has enough capacity and parallelism to absorb the workload. A too-small or over-bottlenecked progress area can make an otherwise mobile design search endlessly without converting organization into victory.\n90. When a solitaire already has adequate card flow and a real sink, repeated search-cap losses with moderate-to-high usage often point to excessive reversible freedom rather than insufficient mobility. Tightening the most permissive tableau rule\u2014especially unrestricted moves to empty piles\u2014can reduce churn and make successful lines easier to find without lowering the game's decision count too far.\n91. When a multi-deck solitaire must ultimately clear the whole tableau, its sink capacity has to scale to the full deck, not just to the number of visible destination piles. Requiring all-at-once completed-set removals can make an otherwise mobile game effectively unwinnable; allowing incremental, irreversible progress often preserves challenge while making search productive.\n92. When a rules engine rejects duplicate source\u2192destination move definitions, merge them into one condition tree before making balance changes. Restoring a runnable move graph with the smallest coherent edit gives much more useful playtest data than redesigning around an uncompiled file.\n93. When playtests show high pile/card usage but only search-cap losses, the problem is often not lack of moves but lack of irreversible progress. Converting one busy pile type from a reversible holding area into a one-way sink aligned with the win condition can reduce churn and make existing organization rules meaningful.\n94. When a no-stock solitaire has occasional 0-move or near-0-move losses despite a solid overall win rate, the problem is often opening contact between tableau tops rather than the endgame sink. A small increase in single-card compatibility can rescue dead starts while leaving stack-building discipline and long-term challenge intact.\n95. When a solitaire variant fails to compile from bad card totals, fix that accounting with the smallest coherent layout change before redesigning rules; otherwise you learn nothing about the game\u2019s actual play.\n96. If every reachable move is reversible or only shifts cards among piles that must all end empty, the game is usually mathematically unwinnable. A solitaire layout needs at least one one-way progress area that the win condition explicitly rewards.\n97. When a game loses after long searches with moderate card usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of an incremental sink. Replacing an all-at-once completion gate with a step-by-step progress pile can turn reversible tableau churn into meaningful advancement without rewriting the whole layout.\n98. When most losses are exhausted after 0\u20135 moves, the bottleneck is often the opening access to the game\u2019s irreversible sink, not overall complexity. A small expansion of what can legally start progress piles can dramatically improve solvability while keeping the rest of the structure intact.\n99. When a rules engine disallows duplicate source\u2192destination move definitions, merge the permissions into one condition tree rather than splitting near-identical rules. Also, if the win condition requires active piles to empty, make sure there is a genuine irreversible destination with enough capacity to hold all cards that must leave them.\n100. When a game already has a one-way accumulation pile, first align the win condition to that existing progress route before adding new mechanics. A solitaire can look \u201chard\u201d in simulation simply because its objective ignores the only irreversible sink and instead demands that temporary holding areas somehow empty themselves.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, and overlapping move-definition conflicts as first-order design failures. Fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve the current pile roles and move graph where possible, restore exact card totals, and re-check that every rule reference and win-condition target is valid in context.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build and opening predicates must be realizable by the actual deck and layout; duplicated decks, reduced decks, or collapsed color distributions can turn standard-looking relations into impossible or overly loose rules.\n- Repair the move graph before balancing challenge. Universal 0-move starts, near-zero usage, or tiny exhausted searches usually mean active piles lack a broadly available opening transfer or cannot reach any real progress route.\n\n## Align card flow, sinks, and victory\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs at least one reachable irreversible sink that the objective explicitly rewards. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying goal.\n- Sink capacity must match the deck and objective. Too few destinations, too little parallelism, duplicated suit-runs without duplicated sink structure, or over-narrow opening rules can make a game mathematically unwinnable even when local mobility looks rich.\n- Prefer incremental, steady progress sinks over all-at-once completion gates. Designs are more solvable when ordinary exposed cards can convert organization into durable advancement step by step.\n- If a pile can receive cards but not release them, it should represent completion, not temporary storage that the game later expects to empty. One-way receiving piles that are not part of victory usually become dead sinks, blockers, or search noise.\n- When a one-way receiving or accumulation area already exists, first consider aligning the win condition to that existing progress route or repurposing it into the main sink before adding a new subsystem.\n- Keep progress irreversible when possible. Easy backflow out of foundations or other sink piles often turns advancement into churn and makes search harder without adding meaningful decisions.\n- Every populated pile type should have a clear role in the win path: expose cards, reorganize them, or accumulate irreversible progress.\n\n## Shape mobility and search space\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use points to missing opening contact or a broken progress path; search-cap losses with moderate-to-high use usually point to excessive reversible freedom or insufficient irreversible progress.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, improve the progress route before adding more mobility. Expanding sink access, capacity, or incrementality usually helps more than adding extra tableau freedom.\n- When search is broad but progress already exists, tighten the busiest reversible routes before adding new ones. Empty-space reuse, permissive helper piles, and broad tableau-to-tableau transfers are common sources of churn.\n- Favor movement rules that reinforce the game\u2019s main structure. Tableau relations and stack moves should organize cards in the same direction that the sink rewards, not create free circulation.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should either be genuinely temporary and clearable, or be converted into constrained completion zones.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small, compatible changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in otherwise workable games, add the narrowest broadly available opening rule that fits the design: a simple tableau relation, a small increase in starting exposure, or a rank-limited move to empties.\n- Prefer constrained empty-pile rules over unrestricted ones. Rank-gated or single-card access to empties often rescues dead starts while preserving planning value and avoiding degenerate shuffling.\n- Small layout shifts can matter when rules are sound: a slight increase in tableau occupancy, starting exposure, or stock feed can improve early contact without flattening the game\u2019s core decisions.\n- If losses split between immediate dead starts and long search churn, suspect \u201crearrangement without resolution\u201d: improve both early contact and the sink\u2019s ability to turn motion into permanent progress.\n\n## Express rules cleanly\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine rejects duplicate source/destination rules, merge related permissions into one coherent condition tree instead of scattering near-duplicates.\n- Use syntax and compile fixes to clarify structure, not just to silence errors: after every repair, verify that the resulting rules still form one coherent source \u2192 tableau/workspace \u2192 sink progression.\n```"}]